  "agent_reply": "The backlog is ready for review. It has no blocking issues and one warning, covered below.\n\n**File:** `/outputs/clinic-test-2/backlog/backlog.md` (data in `backlog/backlog.json`).\n\n**Size:** 10 epics, 79 stories, 484 tasks and 271 story points.\n\n| Epic | Release | Story points |\n|---|---|---|\n| E-001 Platform, sign-in, roles, clinics | R1 | 34 |\n| E-002 Schedules, treatments, free slots | R1 | 29 |\n| E-003 Online booking | R1 | 35 |\n| E-004 Patient appointments | R1 | 26 |\n| E-005 Reception day | R1 | 35 |\n| E-006 Confirmations, reminders, opt-out | R1 | 42 |\n| E-007 Family members | R2 | 18 |\n| E-008 Repeat no-show block | R2 | 19 |\n| E-009 WhatsApp to SMS fallback, failed list | R2 | 23 |\n| E-010 No-show report | R2 | 10 |\n\n**Cross-feature dependencies:** I added 22 story-to-story dependencies across epics, each at confidence 0.65 or higher. Examples are the booking foundation depending on the platform foundation, booking depending on free slots, reminders depending on booking, and the no-show epics depending on reception marking.\n\n**Coverage:** every PRD requirement is covered. NFR-004 (99.5% monthly availability) had no story, so I linked it to the Polish stories of E-001 and E-003. It still has no task of its own, and an open question on S-POLISH-003 asks who owns the availability monitor and alert.\n\n**Warnings and open questions:** the validator shows one warning, that 37 items carry open questions for the reviewer. The main ones are:\n- **Estimates:** every estimate is by LLM at low confidence, because `phase4/history.json` has no resolved tickets to use as analogies.\n- **Priority overrides:** I set all Release 1 stories to P1/must, including ones the specs rated lower, because DL-1 is a hard deadline. Please confirm this for the E-002 leave story (US4) and for the Polish stories. The manager erasure story (US6) and retention job (US7) in E-001 (together 5 SP) come from assumptions, not a PRD requirement. They could drop to should if you want to trim R1 scope.\n- **Messaging:** Q-009 (quiet hours), the unselected SMS/WhatsApp providers, DLT/template approval, and the reading that STOP is read from SMS only (E-006).\n- **Family members:** Q-007 (family members on one phone) is open and affects E-007.\n- **WhatsApp fallback:** the client has not agreed the fallback (ISS-001, E-009).\n- **No-show report:** whether walk-ins count in it (Q-015).\n- **Hosting and domain:** Q-008, which blocks the release-readiness checks in E-001.\n- **Shared code:** ownership of code touched by several epics, such as the slot logic, the booking insert path and migration order.\n\n**Budget:** the backlog is well over the approved Phase 2 estimate. This was the main finding from the enrichers. Every epic exceeds its Phase 2 budget by roughly 2 to 4 times, even reading a person-day as 8 hours. For example, E-001 is 34 SP against 8\u201313, and E-004 is 26 SP against 4\u20137. I did not shrink any numbers to fit. The causes are:\n- tests written first, about 40% of tasks;\n- two repositories;\n- foundation, proof and polish stories;\n- scope outside the Phase 2 estimate.\n\nYou need to either re-baseline the estimate or cut scope before DL-1. The first candidates to cut are the E-001 erasure and retention stories (US6 and US7, 5 SP, assumptions rather than PRD requirements), the E-003 proof stories (US4\u2013US6, 9 SP) and the E-004 message-link story (US4, 2 SP).",
